  "text_md": "Asia Pacific Journal of Multidisciplinary Research, Vol. 5, No. 2, May 2017 _________________________________________________________________________________________________________________________\n\n|Tracing Hopes of Racist-Free and|Asia Pacific Journal of|\n|---|---|\n||Multidisciplinary Research|\n|Multicultural-Friendly Campuses: A|Vol. 5 No.2, 156-162 May 2017|\n|Phenomenological Exploration on the Lived|P-ISSN 2350-7756|\n|Experiences of Blacks in the National Capital Region Universities (Philippines) Zaldy Collado (MS) Adamson University, Manila, Philippines zaldy.collado@adamson.edu.ph Date Received: February 7, 2017; Date Revised: April 27, 2017|E-ISSN 2350-8442 www.apjmr.com|\n\n***Abstract**- In the Philippine campuses nowadays, the presence of foreign students is a common sight,* *making our academic communities one of the most culturally diverse universities in the world. This* *requires us to provide necessary local environment appropriate and ready to attend to multicultural* *sensitivities. Thus the research aimed to look at how well-prepared Philippine universities are in terms of* *having multi-cultural friendly campuses. Through in-depth interviews and phenomenological method, the* *researcher explored the lived experiences of Black African students in their respective campuses. The* *results showed that (a) the participants’ decision to study in the Philippines was based on factors such as* *the hassle-less application process, cheaper yet of quality education and the image of Filipinos as being* *friendly and kind (b) the common, though very light, concern was not racism nor discrimination, but* *speaking in the local language in their presence made them feel sometimes isolated or insulted and (c)* *that racism and discrimination against blacks were not an institutional reality, though unfriendly* *encounters with Filipinos were also recorded, those were not seen as serious cases of racist behavior.* *The study suggested that since blacks’ experiences are generally devoid of racism and discrimination,* *school administrations do not tend to formulate explicit policies and enough activities to ensure racist-* *free campuses and inter-cultural inclusiveness among Filipinos and the blacks. In any case, the black* *students were determined to obtain their degrees here no matter what and such a goal was made easier* *because of the kind of tertiary academic communities their respective schools offer.*\n\n## Keywords–Black Africans, Philippine Universities, Racism\n\n|INTRODUCTION|||sensitivities. But are our campuses really prepared to|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|Philippine|educational|institutions|accept foreign students, especially blacks, into the|\n|marked by an increase in the number of foreign|||arena of social relationships without discrimination|\n|students seeking Philippine diploma. According to|||issues? It is for this reason that the researcher has|\n|Bureau of Immigration Statistics, from 21,000 in|||conducted this study.|\n|2011, the number has tripled in 2012, accounting|||Campus racial discrimination seems to be a|\n|61,000 foreign|students in that year. This sudden||perplexing social issue in the academic settings and|\n|surge of international students is attributed to the|||environments. From different universities and colleges|\n|rising tide of schools which are allowed by the|||(and in the level of governments), the fight against|\n|government to admit international students, which|||this social pathology is continuing and must not stop|\n|from 104 schools in 2011, have ballooned into 2,145|||to ensure further its total eradication in the future and|\n|in 2012. Thus, foreign students walking around|||for future generations. The academic communities of|\n|Philippine campuses are now a common sight, making|||tertiary education, from its teaching and non-teaching|\n|our academic communities one of the most culturally|||personnel, must be responsible enough to pin point|\n|diverse unive